#!|
  Discard patterns, and the exercise that cannot be written from them yet.

  ROADMAP.md Milestone 7 lists discard patterns among the failure classes to
  source a Mushling from, saying each "already has a real compiler
  diagnostic" and to use it verbatim. That is not true of this one. Every
  form below compiles today and the program exits zero:

    - `_` as a match arm, which is the consequential one: a catch-all arm
      makes any match trivially exhaustive, so adding a variant to an enum
      stops forcing anyone to handle it.
    - `val _ = 5`, a binding whose value is discarded outright.
    - `val _unused = 5`, the `_`-prefixed form.

  MANIFESTO.md does not ban them either. The prohibition on `_` lives in
  AGENTS.md, under rules that govern this compiler's own Rust source rather
  than the language it compiles — the two were conflated when the roadmap
  entry was written.

  So this file is not wired into `exercises()`. An exercise teaches a rule by
  showing its diagnostic, and there is no rule here yet to teach. Whether
  Cinnabar rejects discards is a language decision: it belongs in
  MANIFESTO.md first, and the exercise follows the rule rather than the other
  way round.

  Kept as the record of that gap, and as the program to check against once
  the decision is made.
|#

pub type Colour
  Red
  Green
end

pub fun classify(value: Colour) I64
  match value
    Red => return 1
    _ => return 0
  end
end

pub fun main() I64
  val _ = 5
  val _unused = 7
  return classify(Red)
end
